from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-4301';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 1.1: ODD-MAX-BIT and one-sided approximation, Theorem 1 and Conjecture 3 on page 17:4.'
refs += [dict(id='full',title='Lower Bounds for the Approximate Degree of Block-Composed Functions — full revision',authors='Justin Thaler',year=2016,url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2014/150/',pdf_url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2014/150/revision/3/download/',locator='Revision 3, 27 March 2016, latest listed revision. Conjecture 3 in Section 1.1 and its restatement in Section 5. Earlier revisions have different numbering and weaker application bounds.'),dict(id='survey',title='Approximate Degree in Classical and Quantum Computing',authors='Mark Bun; Justin Thaler',year=2022,url='https://doi.org/10.1561/0400000107',pdf_url='https://open.bu.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/10d0eff1-b992-412f-a66b-dd19ceadcc35/content',locator='Foundations and Trends in Theoretical Computer Science 15(3–4):229–423. Accepted manuscript dated 15 December 2022 in Boston University repository; Section 10.5.2 discusses specific OMB compositions and sign-rank applications. Its 2023 repository date is not treated as a new theorem revision.')]
save(i,dict(title='Two-sided hardness amplification by ODD-MAX-BIT',formal=r'Does there exist a universal constant \(c>0\) and an integer \(M_0\ge1\) such that, for every \(N\ge1\), every \(M\ge M_0\), and every total Boolean function \(f:\{-1,1\}^N\to\{-1,1\}\), \(\widetilde{\deg}_{1-2^{-cM}}(\operatorname{OMB}_M\circ f)\ge\widetilde{\deg}_{2/3}(f)\)? Here the composition applies the same f separately to M disjoint blocks of N bits, and OMB returns the parity-coded position of the last −1 output. Both approximate degrees in the proposed inequality are ordinary two-sided, pointwise approximate degrees.',definitions='''For a total function g:{−1,1}ᵗ→{−1,1} and an error parameter η∈[0,1), its η-approximate degree, written d̃_η(g), is the least nonnegative integer d for which a real polynomial p in t variables of total degree at most d satisfies |p(x)−g(x)|≤η for every x∈{−1,1}ᵗ. Coefficients are arbitrary real numbers, with no bound on their magnitudes, bit representations, monomial count or computation time. Polynomials may be taken multilinear on the Boolean cube by replacing even powers of each variable by 1. A constant function has approximate degree zero. The target is a mathematical degree inequality, not a demand to efficiently construct the polynomial or compute its minimum degree from a succinct representation.

For y=(y₁,…,y_M)∈{−1,1}ᴹ, define j=max({i∈{1,…,M}:y_i=−1}∪{0}). Then OMB_M(y)=−1 if j is odd and +1 if j is even. In particular OMB_M(+1,…,+1)=+1. The index runs from 1 to M and “last” means the largest index, matching the primary source. It does not mean parity of the total number of negative inputs. Given disjoint blocks x^(1),…,x^(M)∈{−1,1}ᴺ, define (OMB_M∘f)(x^(1),…,x^(M))=OMB_M(f(x^(1)),…,f(x^(M))). Its domain is the entire cube on MN bits; there are no promises, correlations or overlapping blocks.

The constant c and threshold M₀ are independent of f,N,M and the degree. This spells out the uniform exponential-margin interpretation of 1−2^{−Ω(M)} in Conjecture 3. Only M tends to infinity in that error expression, and N may vary freely with M. The bound must preserve the entire integer degree d̃_{2/3}(f), not merely a fixed fraction of it. Equivalently, whenever d̃_{2/3}(f)≥d for an integer d, no polynomial of degree less than d may approximate the composition within the stated error. The allowance of a universal threshold M₀ expresses the source’s asymptotic notation without asserting a special finite-length constant.

Because outputs are ±1, the number 1−2^{−cM} is an absolute pointwise approximation error. It is close to 1, not close to zero, and is not a probability that an algorithm makes a mistake. For example, a polynomial satisfying this bound must still have the correct sign everywhere and a nonzero margin at least 2^{−cM}, while also staying within the specified error of ±1. Merely requiring the correct sign is threshold degree, a different measure; no fixed margin follows from a threshold representation of unrestricted coefficients.

The already-proved theorem uses positive one-sided approximate degree instead. A positive one-sided η-approximant p satisfies |p(x)+1|≤η when f(x)=−1 and p(x)≥1−η when f(x)=+1, with no upper bound in the second case. Its minimum degree can be strictly smaller than the ordinary two-sided degree. Thus assuming this one-sided degree is at least d is stronger than assuming ordinary approximate degree at least d. Theorem 1 proves the source’s amplification with that stronger hypothesis and error 1−2^{−M}; it does not imply the proposed two-sided-hypothesis statement.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Prove the displayed inequality with constants uniform over all total inner functions and block lengths, or refute the existence of such constants. A negative answer must rule out every proposed universal c and M₀, rather than merely one numerical choice of c. A theorem assuming large positive one-sided degree, restricting f to one example, losing a factor in the preserved degree, or asserting only a threshold-degree bound does not settle the stated conjecture.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Hardness despite a very weak approximation requirement',text='The proposed composition would retain all of an inner function’s constant-error approximate degree even when the outer approximation error approaches 1 exponentially quickly in the number of blocks. This is a different direction from reducing an error parameter toward zero.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The theorem and conjecture have different hypotheses',text='The source proves amplification for positive one-sided approximate degree and explicitly asks whether ordinary two-sided hardness suffices. Conjecture 3 makes that distinction; the saved draft had retained only the sentence introducing it.',citation='full'),dict(title='The outer function is part of the claim',text='The source shows that replacing OMB by OR does not give the same amplification in general. The card keeps OMB’s exact definition and does not replace this question with a generic composition theorem.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Later applications do not give this universal extension',text='The 2022 survey discusses particular OMB compositions in applications to threshold circuits and sign-rank. Those specific constructions and guarantees do not establish the all-inner-functions approximate-degree inequality asked here.',citation='survey')],why='Tests whether a concrete composition can amplify ordinary polynomial-approximation hardness without a one-sided structural hypothesis. Such a theorem would broaden a mechanism underlying separations in learning, quantum query and communication complexity while preserving a sharp quantitative degree bound.',progress=[dict(date='2016-03-27',text='The latest full revision contains the one-sided amplification theorem and retains Conjecture 3 for an ordinary two-sided hardness assumption.',citation='full'),dict(date='2016',text='The ICALP version states Conjecture 3 explicitly on page 17:4.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2022-12-15',text='The later survey manuscript treats particular OMB compositions and their applications; the sections checked do not supply the universal extension in this conjecture.',citation='survey')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the original definitions, Theorem 1 and Conjecture 3, and checked the latest ECCC revision and later survey sections on OMB. Searches through 12 September 2026 did not identify a resolution of the all-total-functions, two-sided version with the full degree preserved. The exponent is stated with universal constants, as the quantitative theorem-style interpretation of the source. Searches are not exhaustive and proofs were not independently audited.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Replaced an introductory extraction fragment with Conjecture 3. Preserved the source’s −1 bit convention, largest-index OMB, disjoint blocks, ordinary pointwise error and exact degree lower bound. Distinguished positive one-sided approximation, threshold degree and the universal constant hidden in the exponent; no proof strategy is included.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A general hardness-amplification question for polynomial approximation that asks whether a significant structural hypothesis can be removed. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The conjecture asks whether ODD-MAX-BIT amplifies the ordinary approximate-degree hardness of every total Boolean function.','The same inner function is evaluated on disjoint blocks, and the outer function reads the parity of the last negative block output.','The composed function must retain the full original degree lower bound even for approximation error exponentially close to one.','The source proves this only under a stronger positive one-sided hardness assumption.','Later specific OMB applications checked here do not settle the universal two-sided extension.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full','survey'])),notes=['Recovered the actual Conjecture 3 and replaced the extraction sentence used as title and statement.','Defined total real-polynomial approximate degree, OMB’s −1 convention, disjoint composition and universal asymptotic constants.','Preserved full degree rather than a constant-factor lower bound and separated error close to one from probability of error.','Checked the March 2016 full revision and the December 2022 survey manuscript; no all-function resolution was found.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
